  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 244 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: GOES-19 Day Cloud Phase Distinction product shows \nextensive cloud cover acrs our cwa this aftn, with some breaks and \nbetter instability north of Montreal ahead of approaching cold \nfront. These breaks have allowed temps to warm into the 70s with sfc \nbased CAPE values around 1000 J/kg, but <300 J/kg over our cwa, due \nto the clouds and lack of sfc heating. As boundary drops southward \ntoward the International Border by 00z, a broken line of showers \nwith a few rumbles of thunder wl be possible near the border, but \nthunder threat should be quickly dissipating around sunset. Have \nlowered qpf amounts by 25 to 35% acrs the board, as precip amounts \nhave been less with minimal instability today, as temps struggle to \nreach 70F. \n\nAfter fropa flow becomes north-northwest upslope with moisture \ntrapped below developing/lowering subsidence inversion, which should \nallow for the development of low stratus type clouds, especially \nacrs the mtns. As subsidence inversion conts to lower toward morning \nand winds become light, thinking some patchy fog wl be possible acrs \nthe trrn, favorable valley and areas that receive >0.10\" of \nrainfall. Fog/low clouds wl slowly burn off by 10 AM Monday with \nincreasing amounts of sunshine expected along with comfortable temps \nin the 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Given the upcoming pattern the predictability of precip \ntiming and placement is low, especially toward Weds/Weds night, as a \nlarge spread remains in latest guidance. Latest guidance indicates \nCAMs are much further north with axis of precip late Monday night \ninto Tues, while larger scale guidance is mostly a miss to our \nsouth. Given the latest trends have cutback on pops Monday \nnight/Tues morning and continued to show highest values south of a \nMSS to BTV to LEB line. Feel most of the precip remains south of our \ncwa thru 12z Tues, given the position of 1024mb sfc high pres nosing \ninto the northern cwa with a developing sharp sw (1.40\") to ne \n(0.45\") pw gradient acrs our cwa. Furthermore, highest instability \nparameters, including elevated CAPE values stay mostly south of the \nNY State Thruway as northwest flow btwn 700-500mb keeps us on the \ncooler side of the boundary into Tues. Given these factors have \ntrimmed back qpf and pops for Monday night into Tues, with highest \nchc/low likely near our cwa border with WFO ALY/BUF. Much lower \nvalues acrs the NEK of VT. \n\nBy Weds potent closed 850 to 500mb cyclonic circulation is progged \nto be north of the Great Lakes, with departing ridge acrs the north \nAtlantic. This wl increase the pres gradient acrs our cwa, with \nincreasing winds likely, along with better chcs for showers and \nthunderstorms, especially on Weds aftn/evening. Height field \nanalysis would suggest strong mid lvl flow btwn low pres to the \nnorth and high pres to the south, question wl be magnitude of \ninstability and timing of best sfc convergence. For now have \ncontinued with NBM for pops with mention of showers and storms, but \npotential for a few strong/severe wl need to be watched.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: An upper level low will remain around heading through \nthe end of the week, so there will be the chance of a few showers \neach day, particularly in the afternoon due to diurnal heating. \nHowever, most of the time it will be dry. Heading into the weekend, \nthe upper low will continue to push east and surface high pressure \nwill build south out of Canada, causing conditions to trend drier. \nHowever, there is uncertainty among the ensembles for when the low \nmoves out and when it becomes completely dry. Currently, \nprobabilities of precipitation during the day are between 10-30 \npercent, mostly driven off EPS members. However, even if these \nwetter solutions verify, the greatest extent of rain would be some \nisolated to scattered showers. Highs should be in the 60s and 70s \nand lows should be in the 40s and 50s.",
